1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,589
Nobody sits down and tells an AI what a

2
00:00:03,589 --> 00:00:07,179
cat looks like. No one writes rules for whiskers

3
00:00:07,179 --> 00:00:10,768
and ears. So how does it learn to recognize

4
00:00:10,768 --> 00:00:14,357
one? By practicing, making mistakes, and correcting itself, again

5
00:00:14,357 --> 00:00:17,947
and again. By the end of this video, you

6
00:00:17,947 --> 00:00:20,739
will understand how a neural network learns.

7
00:00:21,539 --> 00:00:25,265
Imagine a student preparing for an exam. They take

8
00:00:25,265 --> 00:00:28,992
a practice test. They check the answers and see

9
00:00:28,992 --> 00:00:32,718
where they went wrong. Then they adjust how they

10
00:00:32,718 --> 00:00:36,445
study. A neural network learns the same way: guess,

11
00:00:36,445 --> 00:00:37,273
check, adjust.

12
00:00:38,073 --> 00:00:41,653
A neural network is made of layers of simple

13
00:00:41,653 --> 00:00:45,233
units, often called neurons. The first layer takes in

14
00:00:45,233 --> 00:00:48,813
the input, as numbers. The middle layers pass signals

15
00:00:48,813 --> 00:00:52,392
along. The last layer gives the answer. Every connection

16
00:00:52,392 --> 00:00:55,972
between two units has a weight, a number that

17
00:00:55,972 --> 00:00:58,359
says how strong that connection is.

18
00:00:59,159 --> 00:01:02,397
Step one is a guess. We feed in an

19
00:01:02,397 --> 00:01:05,635
example, such as a photo, turned into numbers. The

20
00:01:05,635 --> 00:01:08,873
numbers flow through the layers, and each unit multiplies

21
00:01:08,873 --> 00:01:12,111
what it receives by the weights and adds it

22
00:01:12,111 --> 00:01:15,350
up. At the end, the network gives its guess.

23
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:18,588
At the start of training the weights are random,

24
00:01:18,588 --> 00:01:21,826
so the guess is usually wrong. Here, thirty percent

25
00:01:21,826 --> 00:01:23,625
cat and seventy percent dog.

26
00:01:24,425 --> 00:01:27,440
Step two is to measure the error. We know

27
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:30,454
the right answer, because the example comes with a

28
00:01:30,454 --> 00:01:33,469
label: this is a cat. So we can calculate

29
00:01:33,469 --> 00:01:36,484
a number that says how wrong the guess was.

30
00:01:36,484 --> 00:01:39,498
This number is called the loss, or the error.

31
00:01:39,498 --> 00:01:41,843
A big error means a bad guess.

32
00:01:42,643 --> 00:01:46,027
Step three is to adjust. Using a method called

33
00:01:46,027 --> 00:01:49,411
backpropagation, the network works backwards from the error and

34
00:01:49,411 --> 00:01:52,796
figures out how much each weight contributed to the

35
00:01:52,796 --> 00:01:56,180
mistake. Then each weight is nudged a little, in

36
00:01:56,180 --> 00:01:59,564
the direction that makes the error smaller. After one

37
00:01:59,564 --> 00:02:02,196
nudge, the guess is only slightly better.

38
00:02:02,996 --> 00:02:06,153
Why only a small nudge? Imagine walking down a

39
00:02:06,153 --> 00:02:09,310
foggy hill. You cannot see the bottom, but you

40
00:02:09,310 --> 00:02:12,466
can feel which way the ground slopes. So you

41
00:02:12,466 --> 00:02:15,623
take a small step downhill, and repeat. The network

42
00:02:15,623 --> 00:02:18,779
does the same, where the height of the hill

43
00:02:18,779 --> 00:02:21,936
is the error. This method is called gradient descent.

44
00:02:21,936 --> 00:02:25,092
The size of each step is called the learning

45
00:02:25,092 --> 00:02:25,443
rate.

46
00:02:26,243 --> 00:02:29,827
Now repeat. Show the network another example, guess, measure

47
00:02:29,827 --> 00:02:33,411
the error, and nudge the weights. Do this with

48
00:02:33,411 --> 00:02:36,994
thousands or millions of examples, and the error slowly

49
00:02:36,994 --> 00:02:40,578
falls. The network is not memorizing one picture. It

50
00:02:40,578 --> 00:02:44,162
is finding patterns that work across many. To check,

51
00:02:44,162 --> 00:02:47,745
we test it on new examples it has never

52
00:02:47,745 --> 00:02:48,143
seen.

53
00:02:48,943 --> 00:02:52,528
This is the same learning loop behind large language

54
00:02:52,528 --> 00:02:56,112
models. An image model guesses a label. A language

55
00:02:56,112 --> 00:02:59,697
model guesses the next word. Both are neural networks,

56
00:02:59,697 --> 00:03:03,282
with up to billions of weights, doing the same

57
00:03:03,282 --> 00:03:04,875
thing: guess, check, adjust.

58
00:03:05,675 --> 00:03:08,994
Neural networks are powerful, but they have limits. They

59
00:03:08,994 --> 00:03:12,314
need a lot of examples. They can learn unfair

60
00:03:12,314 --> 00:03:15,634
patterns that are hidden in their data. And it

61
00:03:15,634 --> 00:03:18,953
is hard to explain exactly why a network gave

62
00:03:18,953 --> 00:03:22,273
a particular answer, because the knowledge is spread across

63
00:03:22,273 --> 00:03:23,011
many weights.

64
00:03:23,811 --> 00:03:27,599
Let's recap. One: a network is layers of units

65
00:03:27,599 --> 00:03:31,387
connected by weights. Two: it learns by guessing, measuring

66
00:03:31,387 --> 00:03:35,176
the error, and nudging the weights. Three: it repeats

67
00:03:35,176 --> 00:03:38,964
this over many examples until the error is small.

68
